<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  modules/localization/pom.xml — Stage 2-a "Maven beachhead" module POM.

  STATUS: ADDITIVE and RUN BY HAND (mvn -f modules/localization/pom.xml
  package). modules/localization/build.xml + build.incl.xml (Ant) remains the
  real build; nothing here is wired into CMake or any release build yet.

  WHY THIS MODULE. task-2-brief.md's plan pointed at modules/commons first,
  but commons imports org.scilab.modules.localization (confirmed again here:
  a recursive grep for '^import org.scilab.modules.[a-z_]+' over
  modules/commons/src/java's *.java files finds
  `import org.scilab.modules.localization`) — so it is not a leaf, and a
  beachhead that needs a second module's jar on its classpath is not
  answering the cheap question this stage exists to answer. localization IS a
  leaf: 6 Java files under src/java, zero `import` statements of any kind
  (confirmed: the same recursive grep for '^import ' over
  modules/localization/src/java's *.java files returns nothing). All 6 are
  SWIG-generated JNI stubs (Messages,
  MessagesJNI, LocaleToLCID, LocaleToLCIDJNI, WindowsDefaultLanguage,
  WindowsDefaultLanguageJNI) referencing only java.lang.* (String, System,
  SecurityException, UnsatisfiedLinkError) — no fully-qualified reference to
  any other jar either. Hence: no <dependencies> block below. A module that
  genuinely needed thirdparty/*.jar or another Scilab module's jar would need
  one here (file://-repository / install-file / systemPath scaffolding,
  explicitly temporary — see task-2-brief.md Step 4); localization has
  nothing to resolve, which makes this POM encode ONLY the compiler and
  manifest policy — exactly what the build-parity "jars" dimension measures.

  ONE MORE FILE THAN THE PLAN NAMED: src/main/manifest/MANIFEST-section.MF.
  task-2-brief.md's Files list named only two POMs; this module ended up
  needing a third, small, plain-text file too, added ONLY because
  maven-jar-plugin's <manifestSections>/<manifestEntries> (the mechanism the
  brief specified) turned out not to reproduce the per-package section
  correctly — see the <manifestFile> comment in <build> below for what broke
  and why the fix lives there instead. It carries the exact same section
  content this POM would otherwise have encoded inline; nothing about the
  module's dependency shape changed.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.scilab</groupId>
    <artifactId>scilab-modules-parent</artifactId>
    <version>2027.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>localization</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <!-- build.xml:19 <property name="library.title" value="Localization support"/> -->
  <name>Localization support</name>

  <build>
    <!-- build.incl.xml:20 <property name="src.dir" location="src/java/"/>.
         Points Maven straight at the SAME source tree Ant compiles — no
         files are copied or duplicated into src/main/java; both build tools
         compile the identical .java files on disk. -->
    <sourceDirectory>src/java</sourceDirectory>

    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <!-- addMavenDescriptor=false and the Built-By entry are inherited
                 from the parent POM (scilab/pom.xml) — not redeclared here.
                 Only what is genuinely specific to this module's jar lives
                 below. -->
            <!--
                 THE PER-PACKAGE MANIFEST SECTION IS A RUNTIME DEPENDENCY,
                 NOT COSMETICS. build.incl.xml:148-163 stamps a per-package
                 manifest section (Name: org/scilab/modules/<project>/,
                 carrying Specification-*/Implementation-* attributes) that
                 modules/xcos/src/java/org/scilab/modules/xcos/io/codec/XcosDiagramCodec.java:304-305
                 and
                 modules/xcos/src/java/org/scilab/modules/xcos/io/writer/CustomWriter.java:121
                 read at RUNTIME via
                 Package.getPackage("org.scilab.modules.xcos").getSpecificationVersion()
                 / .getImplementationVersion() — values the JVM populates
                 from exactly this manifest section — and then stamp INTO
                 every saved .xcos diagram file. Maven's default flat
                 manifest compiles fine and would silently change what
                 users' saved diagrams contain the day xcos itself moves to
                 Maven. localization does not read its own manifest this way
                 today, but this beachhead exists to rehearse the shape of
                 the fix on a harmless module BEFORE a module that actually
                 depends on it needs the same treatment done correctly under
                 real stakes.

                 WHY manifestFile INSTEAD OF manifestSections. The obvious
                 tool, maven-jar-plugin's <manifestSections>/<manifestEntries>,
                 was tried first and produces a WRONG jar, two different ways,
                 confirmed by an actual build (task-2-report.md Step 5 shows
                 both failures byte-for-byte):
                   1) Attribute ORDER inside the section came out
                      unpredictable and did not match the XML declaration
                      order, even with declaration order set to Ant's exact
                      sequence, and even after forcing
                      manifestEntries[@implementation=java.util.LinkedHashMap]
                      (javap on maven-archiver's own ManifestSection.class
                      confirms ITS field is a LinkedHashMap; the scrambling
                      happens earlier, in Plexus/Sisu's generic XML-to-Map
                      configurator, before that field is ever populated) — two
                      different configurations produced two different WRONG
                      orders, neither matching Ant's. The jars parity
                      dimension hashes the whole manifest as one ordered text
                      blob, so this alone fails parity.
                   2) The Class-Path value "${manifest.class-path}" (a
                      deliberately-unresolved Ant property, see below) came
                      out of maven-jar-plugin as the literal string "null",
                      not the expected literal "${manifest.class-path}" text —
                      Mojo Map-parameter interpolation resolves an unknown
                      expression to "null" rather than leaving it as-is,
                      unlike Ant's (and the POM model's own) unresolved-stays-
                      literal behavior.
                 <manifestFile> sidesteps both: maven-archiver merges in a
                 pre-written manifest section from a plain text file, parsed
                 and re-serialized via java.util.jar.Manifest/Attributes,
                 which preserve insertion order (this is the SAME class Ant's
                 own jar writer ultimately targets), and the file is never
                 run through Maven property interpolation at all, so
                 "${manifest.class-path}" survives as inert literal text
                 instead of being (mis-)interpolated.

                 ONE ATTRIBUTE DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT of MANIFEST-section.MF:
                 build.incl.xml:157's Implementation-Version, value
                 "${DSTAMP} ${TSTAMP}" (Ant's build date + minute, e.g.
                 "20260719 2038"). It is a real wall-clock value that can
                 never agree between two separate builds, which is exactly
                 why parity/fingerprint.py's _MANIFEST_VOLATILE regex strips
                 any "Implementation-Version: <8 digits> <4 digits>" line
                 from BOTH sides before comparing. A plain text manifestFile
                 cannot embed a live timestamp (no property interpolation
                 reaches it, by design, per the Class-Path reasoning above),
                 and reproducing the ATTRIBUTE's presence with a fake value
                 in the DSTAMP/TSTAMP shape would need extra resource-
                 filtering machinery for zero observable difference: present-
                 and-stripped and absent-and-never-added compare byte-for-
                 byte identical after normalize_manifest() removes the line
                 from Ant's side too. Omitted rather than faked. -->
            <manifestFile>${project.basedir}/src/main/manifest/MANIFEST-section.MF</manifestFile>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
